1. What is Pydantic and why would you use it? <p>Pydantic is a runtime data validation and parsing library driven by Python type hints. You declare the shape of your data as a model, and Pydantic validates input against it. It coerces compatible values and raises a structured <code>ValidationError</code> when the input is invalid. It also serializes models to dicts and JSON and generates JSON Schema.</p> <p>You use it at system boundaries, wherever untrusted data enters: API requests, config, environment variables, message queues and LLM outputs.</p> 2. What changed between Pydantic v1 and v2? <ul> <li>The core was rewritten in Rust (<code>pydantic-core</code>), which makes v2 roughly 5–50× faster.</li> <li>The methods were renamed: <code>.dict()</code> → <code>.model_dump()</code>, <code>.parse_obj()</code> → <code>.model_validate()</code>, <code>.json()</code> → <code>.model_dump_json()</code>.</li> <li><code>class Config</code> became <code>model_config = ConfigDict(...)</code>.</li> <li><code>@validator</code> / <code>@root_validator</code> became <code>@field_validator</code> / <code>@model_validator</code>.</li> <li><code>Optional[X]</code> with no default is now required.</li> <li><code>BaseSettings</code> moved to the separate <code>pydantic-settings</code> package.</li> <li>New tools were added: <code>TypeAdapter</code>, <code>@computed_field</code>, strict mode and better discriminated unions.</li> </ul> 3. Is Pydantic a replacement for mypy? <p>No. Pydantic validates data at runtime, while mypy and pyright check code statically before it runs. They complement each other. Pydantic ships a mypy plugin so the two agree on model signatures.</p> 4. What's the difference between lax mode and strict mode? <p>Lax mode is the default. In lax mode Pydantic coerces sensible inputs, so <code>"42"</code> becomes <code>42</code> for an <code>int</code> field and <code>"true"</code> becomes <code>True</code> for a <code>bool</code>. Strict mode rejects any value that isn't already the right type.</p> <p>You can enable strict mode per model (<code>ConfigDict(strict=True)</code>), per field (<code>StrictInt</code>, <code>Field(strict=True)</code>) or per call (<code>model_validate(data, strict=True)</code>).</p> <pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, ValidationError


class M(BaseModel):
    model_config = ConfigDict(strict=True)
    n: int


try:
    M(n="1")
except ValidationError as e:
    print(e.errors()[0]["type"])  # int_type
</code></pre> 5. How do you make a field optional? <p>Give it a default. <code>x: int | None</code> is required but may be <code>None</code>. <code>x: int | None = None</code> is genuinely optional.</p> 6. Is it safe to use a mutable default like <code>tags: list[str] = []</code>? <p>Yes. Unlike a plain Python class or function signature, Pydantic deep-copies defaults for each instance. For computed defaults, use <code>Field(default_factory=...)</code>.</p>
